        </ul>
      </aside>
      <article class="ins-article">
        <div class="ins-source-note">
          <span class="k">In response to</span>
          <span class="body"><a href="https://www.trendforce.com/research/download/RP260930EO" target="_blank" rel="noopener">3Q26 Automotive SoC and Memory Datasheet (summary page)</a><span class="pub"> &middot; TrendForce</span></span>
        </div>
        <div class="ins-tldr">
          <div class="lbl">Key takeaways</div>
          <p>A September 2026 market research datasheet summary cuts its global vehicle sales forecast to 89.71 million units as China contracts and buyers favor hybrids. Whether the hybrid share holds, and how memory content is revised, are the open questions.</p>
        </div>
        <p class="lede">A <a class="inline" href="https://www.trendforce.com/research/download/RP260930EO" target="_blank" rel="noopener">market research datasheet summary</a> published in late September 2026 lowers its global vehicle sales forecast again, citing a contraction in China and a buyer shift toward hybrid vehicles. The powertrain mix sets how much power, sensing and memory content each vehicle carries. This affects component demand and capacity planning for electronics programs.</p>
        <figure class="ins-figure"><div class="img"><img src="~/images/automotive.webp" alt="Automotive electronics"/></div></figure>
        <h2 id="why-was-the-global-vehicle">Why was the global vehicle sales forecast cut?</h2>
        <p>Weaker sales in China drove the cut, as a sharp contraction there pulled global sales down 2.6% year on year in the second quarter. Sales outside China recovered, and the summary puts the full-year forecast at 89.71 million units, a 2.3% decline.</p>
        <p>A <a class="inline" href="https://www.marklines.com/en/report/forecastsales_202607" target="_blank" rel="noopener">second forecast for light vehicles</a> reaches 89.36 million units, down 3.2%, and places most of its downward revision on China. There, changes to trade-in incentives and the new energy vehicle tax discount held back early-2026 sales.</p>
        <p>A <a class="inline" href="https://www.just-auto.com/?p=195222" target="_blank" rel="noopener">trade report</a> shows June plug-in hybrid sales in China down 27% year on year, while battery electric sales rose 3.6%. The hybrid shift therefore does not look uniform across regions. Only the public summary was readable for this commentary, because the datasheet tables are paywalled.</p>
        <h2 id="how-does-a-hybrid-weighted">How does a hybrid-weighted mix change automotive electronics content?</h2>
        <p>Slower battery electric growth reduces demand for memory-heavy advanced driver-assistance system (ADAS) and cockpit upgrades, according to the summary. Automakers are reviewing designs to remove redundant memory and are delaying planned memory capacity upgrades. Supply of automotive memory has tightened since the second half of 2025.</p>
        <p>Plug-in hybrids typically still use traction inverters and on-board chargers, so power electronics content continues. Ratings and module counts differ from battery electric platforms. Hybrid platforms also keep combustion engine controllers alongside the electric drive, so control unit counts per vehicle can differ from a battery electric design. For board assembly, a change in memory density alters the bill of materials, placement programs and test coverage, so each design revision needs time to qualify.</p>
        <h2 id="watching">What we're watching</h2>
        <p>These open questions will show how the forecast develops and how electronics programs can plan for volume changes:</p>
        <div class="ins-watchlist"><ul><li>Does the hybrid share keep rising if China plug-in hybrid sales stay below prior-year levels, or does the mix vary more by region than the summary suggests?</li><li>Which control units and cockpit domains see lower memory density in new designs, and how soon do automakers confirm those changes?</li><li>Do delayed memory upgrades return as demand when supply eases, or do programs settle on lower densities?</li><li>How do power module and on-board charger volumes per vehicle compare between hybrid and battery electric platforms?</li><li>Do forecasts stabilize after repeated cuts, and what volume range should a program plan around?</li></ul></div>
        <h2 id="buyers">Why it matters for manufacturing buyers</h2>
        <p>A lower global vehicle sales forecast with a hybrid-weighted mix suggests that <a class="inline" href="/markets/automotive">automotive</a> programs may see volumes and memory content change. Hana supports automotive programs in <a class="inline" href="/markets/automotive/power-modules">power modules</a>, <a class="inline" href="/markets/automotive/sensor-assembly">sensor assembly</a> and <a class="inline" href="/markets/automotive/automotive-pcba">PCBA</a>. Where a program dual-sources from multiple countries within the same company, each site is qualified separately against the customer's requirements.</p>
        <section class="ins-sources" id="sources"><h2>Sources</h2><ul><li><a href="https://www.trendforce.com/research/download/RP260930EO" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TrendForce, 3Q26 Automotive SoC and Memory Datasheet (summary page), September 30, 2026</a></li><li><a href="https://www.marklines.com/en/report/forecastsales_202607" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MarkLines, global light vehicle sales forecast, Q2 2026 edition (forecast attributed to GlobalData), July 31, 2026</a></li><li><a href="https://www.just-auto.com/?p=195222" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Just Auto, "China domestic car market retails down by 23% in June," July 10, 2026</a></li></ul></section>
